00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:32.720
Tuscany is a very hilly area. So when

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:35.520
you have hills, you have a natural

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.720
barrier that&#x27;s blocking uh wind from the

00:25:38.720 --> 00:25:41.200
groves. And so as we had seen before in

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:43.200
the harvest, wind is one of like the

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:48.240
main enemies um of an olive early or

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:50.559
later in its stage of production. So

00:25:50.559 --> 00:25:53.520
these hilly areas sort of protect the

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:57.520
trees from from being passed through and

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:00.000
and the olives are falling. Um in

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:03.799
addition, Tuscanyany has a bural

00:26:03.799 --> 00:26:07.440
um like weather pattern and so it has

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.799
very cool evenings and hot days and that

00:26:10.799 --> 00:26:13.559
is essential for the maturity of a

00:26:13.559 --> 00:26:17.679
polyphenol to to be like as healthy as

00:26:17.679 --> 00:26:19.039
possible. So you can have a high

00:26:19.039 --> 00:26:22.320
polyphenol level, but the polyphenols

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:25.919
themselves may not be as rich if this

00:26:25.919 --> 00:26:29.360
flip-flop of cool evenings and hot days

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:32.400
um didn&#x27;t grow them. Um and that&#x27;s the

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.919
same with very hot summers and cool

00:26:34.919 --> 00:26:37.120
winters. Another thing that&#x27;s special

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:38.880
about where we&#x27;re growing is that we&#x27;re

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.919
growing in residential groves. Um, so

00:26:41.919 --> 00:26:45.679
we&#x27;re only sourcing from farmers who are

00:26:45.679 --> 00:26:47.600
actually like growing olives in their

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.080
backyard, which is very special because

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:52.480
normally in large scale productions, you

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:54.960
have groves that are, you know, they

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.640
could be certified organic, but if

00:26:56.640 --> 00:27:00.080
they&#x27;re an industrial area, then then

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:02.200
you risk

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:05.600
um runoff from pesticides, not just via

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:09.520
the soil, but also by air. Um, so where

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:13.600
you grow is super important. Um, and

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.520
olives are a really difficult plant

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:20.080
to grow. It takes about 9 years for a

00:27:20.080 --> 00:27:23.120
tree to start fruing and then another 5

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:26.159
years for that fruit to start producing

00:27:26.159 --> 00:27:28.960
really flavorful, healthy olive oil. So

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:31.360
when you hear about things like olive

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:35.360
oil shortages or um you know high

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.120
quality olive oil being hard to get,

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.360
that&#x27;s because the plant itself just

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:43.200
grows under very specific conditions. So

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:45.600
you want fertile land, but you also want

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.679
a dry place. And so that&#x27;s sort of a

00:27:47.679 --> 00:27:50.000
weird combination, which is why we see

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.159
olives coming out of California,

00:27:52.159 --> 00:27:54.799
Georgia, and then mainly desert or

00:27:54.799 --> 00:27:57.279
Mediterranean areas.

00:27:57.279 --> 00:27:59.919
If you think about grapes, for example,

00:27:59.919 --> 00:28:03.760
when you squeeze a grape, 75% of that

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:07.880
liquid will turn into wine. On a good

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:11.440
harvest, 5 to 15% of an olive will

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:14.159
become oil. So, it&#x27;s it&#x27;s just, you

00:28:14.159 --> 00:28:16.399
know, it exists way less and there&#x27;s

00:28:16.399 --> 00:28:19.279
actually more olive oil being sold in

00:28:19.279 --> 00:28:22.240
the world than amount of olives that

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:26.399
could ever make that oil, unfortunately.

00:28:26.399 --> 00:28:29.039
Um, okay. So, when you pick your olives

00:28:29.039 --> 00:28:31.799
is a really essential part of the

00:28:31.799 --> 00:28:34.640
journey. You want to have an early

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:36.480
harvest. When you hear the term early

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:38.640
harvest, it depends on the region, but

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.120
in Tuscanyany that means early to mid

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:43.320
October is when you&#x27;re beginning to

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:46.840
pick. And that is a direct tradeoff

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:50.399
between quality and quantity. The longer

00:28:50.399 --> 00:28:52.880
you allow your olives to grow for, the

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.039
more liquid that it accrrews inside of

00:28:55.039 --> 00:28:57.679
the olive. And so you will be able to

00:28:57.679 --> 00:29:00.320
extract more oil and sell more oil. But

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:03.120
the oil that you are getting has less

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:05.440
flavor and it has a smaller polyphenol

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:08.360
content. By the way, polyphenols are the

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:11.120
antioxidant in olive oil that that makes

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:12.840
it um

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:15.840
anti-cancer, anti-liver disease and

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:19.039
things like that. So we pick just as the

00:29:19.039 --> 00:29:22.799
olives are turning from green to purple.

00:29:22.799 --> 00:29:26.679
Um and and there are other factors. For

00:29:26.679 --> 00:29:29.679
example, California deals with

00:29:29.679 --> 00:29:32.159
irrigation issues and and droughts and

00:29:32.159 --> 00:29:34.320
water issues in a way that Tuscany may

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.440
not. But Tuscany deals with the fly. So

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.679
if your olives aren&#x27;t quite ready, but

00:29:39.679 --> 00:29:43.039
the fly is out, your olives need to be

00:29:43.039 --> 00:29:46.799
ready. Um, and so various factors such

00:29:46.799 --> 00:29:50.240
as wind, rain, there can be too much

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.000
rain that year, there can be not enough

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:53.679
rain that year, and you may have a later

00:29:53.679 --> 00:29:56.200
pick. That all depends on the

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:59.279
timing, how we pick. So, when you&#x27;re

00:29:59.279 --> 00:30:01.039
picking olives, you want to be as gentle

00:30:01.039 --> 00:30:02.960
as possible because when you think of a

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.600
fruit, when a fruit cracks open, it&#x27;s

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:07.520
it&#x27;s going to start rotting. You

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:09.520
wouldn&#x27;t want to make a juice out of

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.840
like an apple that&#x27;s, you know, cracked

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.760
open and has been on the counter all

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:15.840
day. But unfortunately, like that is

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:18.679
what happens a lot with olive oil.

00:30:18.679 --> 00:30:23.360
Um, but in order to to protect like a

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:26.159
somewhat fragile fruit as olives, uh,

00:30:26.159 --> 00:30:29.600
you want to hand pick or use scrapers,

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:31.600
which essentially like will comb the

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:35.039
trees in a in an electronic way. uh

00:30:35.039 --> 00:30:36.720
they&#x27;ll sort of like buzz and zoom

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:38.799
through the trees and the olives will

00:30:38.799 --> 00:30:43.279
drop onto a net and then we manually um

00:30:43.279 --> 00:30:45.760
move the nets. We bring the nets into

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:48.159
baskets and they&#x27;re onto the next tree.

00:30:48.159 --> 00:30:50.080
Unfortunately, what&#x27;s like very common

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:52.320
and and very efficient is using

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.720
tractors. Just sort of like bulldoze

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:58.080
through olive groves. They shake the

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:01.360
trees, the olives will fall onto the um

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:04.480
to the nets. often they will crack and

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:07.440
then that will be um what makes the

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:10.000
olive oil. So you just want to think as

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:12.960
gently as possible um is how you want to

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:14.039
handle

00:31:14.039 --> 00:31:15.799
olives.

00:31:15.799 --> 00:31:19.600
Um when you press olive oil is probably

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.559
like my favorite indicator of quality

00:31:22.559 --> 00:31:24.559
that typically doesn&#x27;t make it onto the

00:31:24.559 --> 00:31:27.360
label. Hopefully a label will indicate

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:30.720
like extra virgin, whether it&#x27;s organic,

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:32.720
um, and the harvest date so that you can

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:34.960
know if it was an early harvest or not,

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:36.960
which by the way will vary based on

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:40.159
country. So like a Greek production will

00:31:40.159 --> 00:31:43.600
be later in the year than Italy. And

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:46.240
even within Italy, southern regions will

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:49.240
begin their harvest first and then

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:52.399
um as early as like late September and

00:31:52.399 --> 00:31:56.000
then um and then Tuscanyany and then

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.960
even further north. Um, but so when you

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:02.960
press, so we press our olives twice a

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:05.440
day, typically within two hours of

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:07.480
picking the olives, which is really

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.919
special. Typically, again, like when

00:32:09.919 --> 00:32:12.080
you&#x27;re in a big large scale production

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:14.880
and you&#x27;re trying to make money, you may

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:17.120
wait until the end of the week or the

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:19.279
end of the season or the end of the

00:32:19.279 --> 00:32:21.840
month to bring your olives to the

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:24.480
frantoo or the the press. Uh, that&#x27;s the

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:27.600
Italian word for press. um because

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.159
there&#x27;s overhead costs associated with

00:32:30.159 --> 00:32:34.080
going to the press. So if you&#x27;re um

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:35.760
pressing twice a day, you&#x27;re able to

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:37.760
capture the polyphenols, again, the

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:40.960
flavor. Um and you know, that&#x27;s a little

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:42.960
more expensive. You&#x27;re using more fuel,

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:44.559
you&#x27;re taking away from labor, but

00:32:44.559 --> 00:32:46.880
that&#x27;s one of the best indicators of

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:50.399
knowing how uh fresh your olive oil is

00:32:50.399 --> 00:32:53.360
and how long the polyphenols will last.

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:55.679
Um, so we&#x27;re getting a bit obsessive

00:32:55.679 --> 00:32:59.000
here, but um, but that&#x27;s super

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:01.240
fun.

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:06.000
Um, okay. Um, so where we press. So when

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.480
you&#x27;re in Italy, uh, for the olive

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:10.000
season, it&#x27;s kind of like being in New

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:13.360
York with apple picking. Um, everyone&#x27;s

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:15.440
at the press. There&#x27;s hundreds and

00:33:15.440 --> 00:33:17.519
hundreds of families and people and

00:33:17.519 --> 00:33:18.960
people in the village going to the

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:22.399
presses bringing their olives and um you

00:33:22.399 --> 00:33:23.679
know it&#x27;s quite the scene. It&#x27;s a

00:33:23.679 --> 00:33:25.919
cultural moment but that means that

00:33:25.919 --> 00:33:28.640
everyone else&#x27;s olives will affect your

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:30.720
olive oil because you can&#x27;t clean the

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:34.080
machine with soap. So what happens is if

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:36.240
you&#x27;ve treated your olives really well

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:38.720
but your neighbor hasn&#x27;t for example or

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.799
if they said that it was organic but

00:33:40.799 --> 00:33:43.279
they&#x27;re not. um that&#x27;s all passing

00:33:43.279 --> 00:33:47.279
through the same machinery. Um so in my

00:33:47.279 --> 00:33:50.440
case, I go to a private press.

00:33:50.440 --> 00:33:53.039
Basically, the mayor of the village

00:33:53.039 --> 00:33:56.159
caught wind of this project and was very

00:33:56.159 --> 00:33:58.320
excited about it and wanted to set us up

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:00.720
with someone who had like their own

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:02.799
machinery. And so we know in that

00:34:02.799 --> 00:34:05.120
instance it&#x27;s their olives and our

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:07.079
olives and and no one

00:34:07.079 --> 00:34:08.679
else&#x27;s.

00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:12.879
Um okay. And then how you press is also

00:34:12.879 --> 00:34:15.119
a really important factor. So if you&#x27;ve

00:34:15.119 --> 00:34:19.599
seen um you know olden day imagery of

00:34:19.599 --> 00:34:21.760
olive oil making in Italy, you may see

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:24.480
like the large stone grinders that sort

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:27.520
of like spin in a circle and crush

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:31.040
olives and the olives will like emit

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:34.240
their juice that way. Um but a more

00:34:34.240 --> 00:34:37.839
modern way is using uh a mechanical

00:34:37.839 --> 00:34:40.720
machine or using a two-phase con system

00:34:40.720 --> 00:34:43.599
called continua which essentially means

00:34:43.599 --> 00:34:46.800
that you are able to extract the liquid

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:50.720
without using excess water which again

00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:54.560
water um in that instance will further

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:57.119
along the oxidation process of the oil

00:34:57.119 --> 00:35:00.760
which then disrupts the the polyphenol

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:04.720
count. Um, storing olive oil is also a

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:08.800
really important factor. So, the enemy

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:12.400
of olive oil is air and sunlight. So,

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:14.880
you never want to store your olive oil

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:17.280
near anything having to do with heat in

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.680
a factory. You don&#x27;t want to be near

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:21.280
actually too close to the machinery

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:24.160
because of the heat that it will emit.

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:26.560
Um, and you want to use like these

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:29.200
thick, heavy, expensive stainless steel

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:32.640
containers, um, which are hard to find,

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:35.440
but that&#x27;s what will protect the oil in

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:38.560
the 2 days that you want to hold off

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:40.400
from bottling so that some of the

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:43.680
sediments can separate from

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:47.520
um, from the oil and and not make it

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:49.960
into the bottles.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:53.839
Um, so shipping is another really big uh

00:35:53.839 --> 00:35:57.119
factor for quality. So if you&#x27;re dealing

00:35:57.119 --> 00:36:02.079
with um boats and shipping by sea, then

00:36:02.079 --> 00:36:04.640
a big concern is where your cargo is on

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:07.920
the boat because whether it&#x27;s below the

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:10.400
water line or above the water line will

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:13.480
affect the heat of the olive oil and

00:36:13.480 --> 00:36:16.400
therefore the quality, how quickly it&#x27;s

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:19.280
arriving. Um, so we overnight air

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:21.280
freight our olive oil so that people can

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:23.280
try it like straight off the branch. So

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:25.680
it&#x27;s exciting that

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:27.440
uh what the oil that you&#x27;ll be trying

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:29.880
tonight is about 5 months off of the

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:33.359
branch. Typically by the time olive oil

00:36:33.359 --> 00:36:35.119
moves through the supply chain, it&#x27;s

00:36:35.119 --> 00:36:37.280
already 1 to two years old once like

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:40.119
it&#x27;s marketed as fresh.

00:36:40.119 --> 00:36:45.200
Um, so you can work all season to ensure

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:47.520
that you know your machinery is clean

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:49.839
and you&#x27;re organic and you picked early,

00:36:49.839 --> 00:36:52.240
but really by the time it gets to

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:54.560
people, it just may not be what it

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:55.800
originally was

00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:59.599
anymore. Um, and then olive varietals.

00:36:59.599 --> 00:37:01.599
So, olive oil, a lot of people don&#x27;t

00:37:01.599 --> 00:37:04.560
expect is really similar to like wine or

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:07.839
grapes in that every single variety will

00:37:07.839 --> 00:37:11.119
make the oil tastes and feel a really

00:37:11.119 --> 00:37:15.040
different way. So, in the olive oil that

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.720
you&#x27;ll try today, it&#x27;s made mainly with

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:21.359
or the the filtered from Florence is

00:37:21.359 --> 00:37:24.320
made mainly with rantoo and morio

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:27.680
olives. So those olives are prized for

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:29.760
their like grassy flavor, their high

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:32.400
polyphenol count. Um so they&#x27;re more

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:34.880
expensive and actually a lot of the

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:36.480
farmers laughed at me when I said like

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:38.000
those are the olives that I wanted to

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.960
use because they were like why would you

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:42.320
we&#x27;re going to charge you more for that

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:44.079
and Americans really don&#x27;t know the

00:37:44.079 --> 00:37:47.280
difference. Um but that&#x27;s sort of you

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:48.880
know the point of this and what I&#x27;m

00:37:48.880 --> 00:37:51.920
going for. every single varietal um has

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.920
a different flavor and every varietal

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:56.880
also has its role in the field. So in

00:37:56.880 --> 00:38:00.160
the olive oil industry, it&#x27;s very trendy

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:03.200
to have like a mono cultivar or using a

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:07.839
single type of olive in the oil. Um but

00:38:07.839 --> 00:38:10.320
that&#x27;s actually not ideal for the grove.

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:15.599
The grove wants um a a diverse amount of

00:38:15.599 --> 00:38:18.720
olives. For example, the pendolino olive

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.440
is the cross-pollinator olive. So if one

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:23.599
tree is struggling

00:38:23.599 --> 00:38:25.760
uh during the season, the pendolino

00:38:25.760 --> 00:38:28.320
olive will actually send nutrients

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:31.040
through the wind to other trees. And so

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:35.400
each varietal has a certain role in that

00:38:35.400 --> 00:38:39.280
regard. And in the um second olive oil

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:41.839
that we have tonight, the unfiltered

